## 2.1 Cloudflare R2 protection

The existing website uses Cloudflare R2 to store and serve uploaded media.

You must:

1. Preserve the existing R2 bucket and object paths.
2. Preserve all existing image URLs.
3. Preserve the existing image upload workflow.
4. Preserve the R2 custom domain and image delivery configuration.
5. Preserve existing directory structures.
6. Preserve image metadata and references.
7. Preserve existing image selection and assignment.
8. Never delete or overwrite R2 objects.
9. Never migrate images to another storage provider.
10. Never replace existing images with Unsplash, stock photography, AI-generated images, or placeholders.

Do not modify the image upload system unless a change is strictly necessary to prevent existing functionality from breaking. Any such change must preserve all stored media.

## 2.2 Firestore image protection

Images uploaded through the admin panel are associated with website sections through Firestore.

These references must remain intact.

Do not overwrite existing Firestore documents with default CMS content.

Do not reset existing CMS records.

Do not replace administrator-selected images with hardcoded default image paths.

Do not change image field values when updating text.

If a document contains both text and image data, update only the relevant text fields.

Prefer non-destructive field-level updates.

Avoid whole-document replacements that could accidentally remove image fields.

## 2.3 Preserve image assignments during UI changes

When restructuring an existing section:

- Keep its current image.
- Keep its current image URL.
- Keep its existing media reference.
- Preserve its connection to the correct section.
- Preserve its mobile and desktop image assignments.
- Preserve existing image focal points and cropping settings where stored.

You may change the surrounding typography, spacing, layout, borders, or animation behavior.

However, do not alter the underlying image asset, its CMS assignment, or administrator-defined display settings.

If a section is moved, its image must move with it.

If a section is renamed, its existing image reference must remain connected.

If an existing section is split into multiple sections, preserve the original image in its original content context. Do not automatically duplicate or redistribute the image.

If a section is merged with another, preserve all existing image references without overwriting them.

## 2.4 New sections

If a new section requires an image but no existing image has been assigned:

- Do not automatically generate an image.
- Do not insert random stock photographs.
- Do not borrow an image from an unrelated section.
- Do not replace an existing image.
- Do not invent a new R2 URL.

Instead, create an image-free layout or an optional CMS image field that the administrator can populate later.

The section must remain visually attractive even without an image.

## 2.5 Mandatory image audit

Before making any changes, identify all existing image fields, references, and assignments.

Create a baseline inventory of image references and their associated sections.

After implementation, compare the final state with the baseline.

Every previously assigned image must remain assigned to its original content.

**Image preservation is more important than visual redesign.**

If a proposed UI change would risk losing an existing image assignment, redesign the UI change instead.

